Except for the negative connotations of the word "ghetto"
(free-software advocates would generally prefer a word more
like "democracy"), this is in fact *exactly* what the GPL
is specifically designed to do.  The people who designed
the GPL were very deliberate and painstaking in their
effort to ensure that it was carefully worded in such a way
as to do precisely this.  In principle, pretty much any
share-alike license will have the same effect.

People who want the software they write to be freely usable
by everyone and adaptable to these kinds of situations are
better served by releasing it under a more permissive
license (BSD, MIT, X11, etc.)  Then these sorts of things
aren't a problem.

The GPL is not about ensuring that everyone can use the
software you wrote.  If that's all you want, you can just
release your software into the public domain, and Bob is
your uncle.  Everyone is free to use it.

The GPL is about ensuring that nobody can EVER use or
distribute the software you wrote in a manner that is in
any way inconsistent with the FSF's principles.  That's the
whole point of the GPL.


